<h2>Our purpose</h2><p>World Food Crisis helps readers understand what food-system numbers can—and cannot—tell them. We cover food prices, harvests, fertilizer, trade, reserves, storage, market access and acute food insecurity. The aim is to explain evidence without turning uncertainty into alarm or confusing a forecast with a confirmed outcome.</p>
<h2>Who writes the articles?</h2><p>Articles are published under the <strong>World Food Crisis Editorial Desk</strong> byline. This is an editorial byline for the website, not a named individual or a claim of review by an external agency. We do not invent individual author credentials or imply that FAO, WFP, IPC, WHO or any other cited organization reviewed or endorsed our articles.</p><p>Each article links to original sources where possible and shows a publication or update date. Country briefings identify the geographic coverage and reference period of the source being discussed. A source link means we use that material as evidence; it does not imply that the source endorses our interpretation.</p>
<h2>How we choose and explain data</h2><ul><li>Prefer primary publications and official datasets for important claims.</li><li>State the release date, reference period, geography and definition for key numbers.</li><li>Separate observed results, estimates, forecasts, scenarios and our interpretation.</li><li>Explain when statistics measure different things, such as commodity prices, household inflation, production, or acute food insecurity.</li><li>Update pages when meaningful new evidence appears, and preserve context for older projections.</li></ul>
<h2>How to read our bylines and dates</h2><p>The editorial desk byline identifies the publisher responsible for the page. “Updated” means the page content or data context was checked or changed on that date; it does not mean every source figure refers to the same day. The date attached to a statistic is its source's release date or reference period, stated separately where possible.</p>
<h2>Corrections and contact</h2><p>If you identify an error, send the page URL, the exact claim and a primary source that supports the correction. See <a href="/methodology/">our editorial methodology</a> and <a href="/contact/">contact and corrections</a>. A monitored public editorial email address still needs to be configured for this project; we do not publish a fabricated address.</p>
<h2>Limits</h2><p>This is an educational publication, not a live emergency service, a food-assistance provider or a substitute for local official guidance. Country analyses cover defined locations and time periods and can be superseded. For urgent food assistance or safety instructions, consult relevant authorities and established humanitarian organisations.</p></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="container footer-main"><div class="footer-brand"><a class="brand brand-footer" href="/"><span class="brand-mark">F<span>+</span></span><span class="brand-words"><strong>World Food Crisis</strong><small>Facts before fear.</small></span></a><p>Independent explainers on food availability, prices and food security.</p></div><div class="footer-col"><strong>Explore</strong><a href="/data/">Data desk</a><a href="/sources/">Sources</a><a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a></div><div class="footer-col"><strong>About</strong><a href="/about/">Our purpose</a><a href="/contact/">Corrections & contact</a><a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a></div></div><div class="container footer-bottom"><span>© <span data-year>2026</span> World Food Crisis</span><span>Sources over speculation.</span></div></footer></body></html>